  "query_preview": "/SATURDAY THROUGH THURSDAY/...\nIssued at 109 PM MST Thu Nov 26 2026\n\nFriday night through Saturday night...\n\nPotent disturbance in the NW Flow will move across the region. This \nwill some accumulating snow to the central mtns. Additionally, it \nwill bring increasing winds to all of the higher terrain. \n\nHeaviest snow will fall on Saturday over the central mtns. Winds of \n50 to 60 mph will be likely so travel in the central mtns will be \ndifficult. Overall snow amounts are expected to be modest, with 4 to \n6 inches likely. most of the snow over the central mtns will fall \nnorth of Cottonwood Pass. \n\nThe San Luis Valley and SW mtns should remain dry Saturday but it \nwill be windy.\n\nAs for the plains, Saturday will be mild and breezy. The front \nshould cross the region during mid afternoon time period with strong \nnortherly gusts likely by later in teh day. Winds of 45 to 55 mph \nwill be likely. We may see some gusts reaching high wind criteria \nlate afternoon/evening over the far eastern plains.\n\nAs for precip on the plains, we will likely see rain/snow over the \nPalmer Divide by late morning into the evening hours. Some of this \nprecip will be possible over the southern I-25 corridor region by \nevening. Some guidance members show some light showers over the far \neastern plains.\n\nSunday...\n\nMuch cooler all areas. Max temps will likely only be in the 40s to \naround 50 over the plains and larger valleys, but it will be dry. \nWinds all areas will be significantly less than Saturday.\n\nMonday and Tuesday...\n\nDry most areas with milder temps returning. The only area which we \nmay see some shower will be over the central mtns where some snow \nshowers will be possible. \n\nWednesday and Thursday...\n\nGuidance shows another disturbance is fcst to move through. However \nthe brunt of the energy on latest 12Z guidance is showing most of the \nforcing will move across N New Mexico, so best chances of precip \nwill likely occur along and south of US50. \\/Hodanish",
